# linkrot

Find dead, moved, and blocked links in any text: a README, a bookmarks
export, loose notes, a list of URLs.

It comes as two commands sharing one core:

- `linkrot`: a CLI built for scripts and CI (exit codes, JSON, stdin)
- `linkrot-tui`: an interactive terminal UI with live results

```
$ linkrot README.md
BROKEN   404  https://example.com/old-page  (not found)
ERROR    ---  https://gone-forever.example/  (domain not found, DNS lookup failed)
BLOCKED  403  https://some-site.example/  (server refused us; may still work in a browser)
REDIRECT 200  http://example.com/docs  (permanently moved)
              -> https://example.com/docs/
OK       200  https://example.com/

42 links checked: 38 ok, 1 redirect, 1 blocked, 1 broken, 1 error
```

## The five outcomes (and why there are five, not two)

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **OK** | Answered 2xx directly |
| **REDIRECT** | Works, but only after being redirected. The address has moved; update it before the redirect itself rots. Marked "permanently moved" when any hop was a 301/308 |
| **BROKEN** | The server answered and said no: 404, 410, 5xx |
| **BLOCKED** | 401/403/429 and friends. The server refused *us*, usually because we look like a bot. The page may be perfectly alive for a human |
| **ERROR** | Never got an HTTP answer: DNS failure, timeout, TLS error, redirect loop. The classic "whole site is gone" |

The split between **BLOCKED** and **BROKEN** is the most important design
decision here. Naive checkers lump every non-200 together and bury you in
false alarms from sites that merely dislike scripts. Reporting "I can't
tell" honestly is more useful than a scary red row that's wrong.

## Install

Needs Python 3.10+.

```bash
# Recommended on Arch/CachyOS: pipx keeps CLI tools isolated.
sudo pacman -S python-pipx
cd link-rot-checker
pipx install -e .      # -e = editable: edits to the code take effect immediately
```

Plain pip in a virtualenv also works:

```bash
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e .
```

That creates the `linkrot` and `linkrot-tui` commands, runnable from any
directory. (This replaces the manual `chmod +x` + symlink dance from the
earlier projects: the `[project.scripts]` section of `pyproject.toml`
tells the installer to generate those launchers for you.)

## Usage

```bash
linkrot README.md                    # every link in a file
linkrot notes.md bookmarks.html      # several files at once
linkrot https://example.com/a        # URLs given directly
cat links.txt | linkrot              # stdin
linkrot README.md --only-bad         # hide OK results
linkrot README.md --json             # machine-readable
linkrot README.md --strict           # REDIRECT/BLOCKED also fail the run
linkrot README.md --timeout 5 --concurrency 50 --per-host 2
linkrot-tui                          # interactive UI
```

TUI keys: `Ctrl+R` check, `Ctrl+L` clear, `Ctrl+Q` quit. Paste text into
the box or type a file path and press Enter to load it. Results stream in
live, then settle into worst-first order.

### Exit codes (so it can gate CI)

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 0 | No broken links |
| 1 | At least one BROKEN or ERROR (with `--strict`, also REDIRECT/BLOCKED) |
| 2 | Bad usage or unreadable input |

Example GitHub Actions step that fails a PR when docs contain dead links:

```yaml
- run: pipx install . && linkrot README.md docs/*.md --only-bad
```

## How it works

### Finding URLs in messy text (`extract.py`)

A URL has no clear end marker, and the text around it is full of
punctuation that looks like part of it:

```
See https://example.com/page.            <- trailing "." ends the sentence
[docs](https://example.com/a_(b))        <- final ")" closes the markdown link
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foo_(bar)  <- but THIS ")" belongs to the URL
```

So it matches greedily, then trims: trailing punctuation goes, and a
trailing `)` is only stripped if it's *unbalanced* (more `)` than `(`),
which handles the markdown and Wikipedia cases correctly. Duplicates are
collapsed, so one dead link repeated 20 times is one row.

### Checking one link (`checker.py`)

1. **HEAD first**: asks for headers only, so no page gets downloaded.
2. **Fall back to GET** if HEAD returns 4xx/5xx. A surprising number of
   servers mishandle HEAD (answering 405, 403, even 404) while the same
   URL works fine with GET. Trusting HEAD alone reports healthy pages as
   dead. The GET is streamed, so only the status line and headers are
   read, never the body.
3. **Retry once on timeout** only. Timeouts are often transient; DNS
   failures and refused connections won't change on retry.
4. **Classify** (see the table above).

### Checking thousands without being rude (`check_all`)

Two separate limits:

- `--concurrency` (default 20): total requests in flight. One at a time
  is painfully slow; all at once exhausts file descriptors.
- `--per-host` (default 3): requests in flight to any *single* site. A
  README with 80 links to one domain would otherwise hit that server with
  80 simultaneous requests: impolite, and a fast way to get rate-limited,
  which would then show up as false BLOCKED results.

An `on_result` callback fires as each link finishes, which is what lets
the TUI show live progress instead of a frozen screen.

## Limitations (honest ones)

- **Soft 404s.** Some sites answer `200 OK` with a "page not found"
  message in the body. A status-code checker can't see that; detecting it
  means reading and judging page content.
- **JavaScript-rendered pages.** We never run JS, so single-page apps that
  decide at runtime whether a route exists will look healthy.
- **`#fragments` aren't verified.** `page.html#section` is checked as
  `page.html`; whether the anchor exists isn't tested.
- **BLOCKED is a shrug, not a verdict.** Open those in a browser to be sure.
- **Only `http(s)://` links** are extracted; relative links (`../docs`) and
  `mailto:` are ignored, since there's no base to resolve them against.

## Extending it

- **`--base-url` for relative links**: resolve `../docs/x.md`-style links
  against a site root, so you can check real HTML pages.
- **Check a live page**: fetch a URL, extract its links, check those
  (a one-level crawler).
- **Wayback Machine suggestions**: for each BROKEN link, query the
  Internet Archive's availability API and print an archived copy. Turns a
  complaint into a fix.
- **Result caching**: remember last-checked status so re-runs skip links
  that were fine an hour ago.
- **Soft-404 heuristics**: flag 200 responses whose title/body says
  "not found".

## Project layout

```
link-rot-checker/
├── pyproject.toml        # packaging + the two console commands
├── README.md
└── linkrot/
    ├── __init__.py
    ├── __main__.py       # enables `python -m linkrot`
    ├── extract.py        # find URLs in messy text
    ├── checker.py        # async checking + classification (the core)
    ├── cli.py            # `linkrot`
    └── tui.py            # `linkrot-tui` (Textual)
```
